, 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \nYou currently hold the **6♦ 5♦**, and the board is **5♣ A♣ 3♣ 2♥ 6♠**. This gives you two strong holdings:\n\n- **Two Pair (6s and 5s)**: You have a pair of 6s (6♦, 6♠) and a pair of 5s (5♦, 5♣), which makes a strong Two Pair hand.\n- **Potential Flush Draw (Club flush draw)**: There are three clubs on the board (A♣, 5♣, 3♣), so any club completes a flush. You have the 6♦ and 5♦ — neither is a club, so you do not have a flush draw in diamonds. However, if your opponent is holding a club, they may be drawing to a flush.\n\nBut note: The board has **three clubs**, so there is a **flush threat**. Also, an opponent could have A-K or A-Q for top pair with a strong kicker, or even A-3/A-5 for two pair themselves.\n\nHowever, **you have a full house draw** as well: any 5 or 6 improves your hand:\n- Another 5 would give you **Three 5s and a pair of 6s** → Full House.\n- Another 6 would give you **Three 6s and a pair of 5s** → Full House.\n\nBut since all cards are already out (river completed), no more cards will come. This is the **River round**, so no further community cards.\n\nThus, your final hand is: **6♦ 5♦ + 5♣ A♣ 3♣ 2♥ 6♠** → Best five-card hand: **6♠ 6♦ 5♣ 5♦ A♣** → **Two Pair (6s and 5s), Ace kicker**.\n\nThis is a very strong hand — only beaten by:\n- Any **Flush** (especially Club flush: any player holding two clubs like K♣Q♣, Q♣J♣, etc.)\n- **Straight**: Not possible here (no five consecutive ranks).\n- **Full House**: Would require opponent to have 5-5 or 6-6 (unlikely), or paired the board in pocket Aces (A-A), but A-A would have been strong earlier.\n- **Four of a Kind**: Impossible on this board.\n\nSo, what can beat you?\n- **Any two club cards giving a flush** — that’s the main danger.\n\nBut consider: your opponent checked through all rounds. On flop (Round 1), they raised 100 after you checked, then you called. Then on turn and river, both players checked. That suggests weakness.\n\nThey raised on the flop (when board was A♣ 5♣ 3♣), which could indicate:\n- Top pair (A-x with club kicker)\n- A set (unlikely without betting more)\n- Or a flush draw (hoping to take pot uncontested)\n\nBut now, on the river, they **checked again**, meaning they likely missed their flush draw or are slow-playing. But given the action, slow-playing a flush seems unlikely — most players bet or raise with a made flush.\n\nTherefore, it\'s plausible that:\n- Opponent doesn\'t have a flush.\n- They may have Ax (weaker kicker), or a weak club draw that missed.\n\nYour **Two Pair (6s and 5s)** with Ace kicker is likely the best hand.\n\nNow, valid actions: [-2, -1, 0, 100, or higher].\n\nSince opponent checked to you on the river (implied from your ability to check or bet), you have option to **check behind** (0) or **bet/raise** to extract value.\n\nBut looking at history:\n- Round 2 (Turn): You checked, opponent checked → went to river.\n- Now on River: It\'s implied opponent checked again (otherwise you wouldn’t have option to bet).\n\nSo yes — you’re first to act on river? Wait — let’s re-express the timeline.\n\nWait: The game history says:\n- Round 0 (Pre-flop): You acted → 0 (Check), Opponent → 0 (Check)\n- Round 1 (Flop): You → 0 (Check), Opponent → raised by 100, You → 0 (Call)\n- Round 2 (Turn): You → 0 (Check), Opponent → 0 (Check)\n→ So now it\'s **River**, and it\'s **your turn to act first** again.\n\nOpponent has just checked on Turn, so likely checks on River too? No — we don’t know yet. But the system says “Your turn”, so **you act first on the River**.\n\nGiven that, you must decide whether to:\n- Check (0): Allow opponent to possibly bluff or check back.\n- Bet (e.g., 100 or more): For value or to deny equity.\n\nBut since opponent has shown passivity after flop raise, they may have:\n- Flop raise with Ax (A♣K♣, A♣Q♣, etc.), or\n- Semi-bluffed with flush draw and missed.\n\nNow, betting for value: Can you get called by worse?\nPossibly:\n- A-high with weaker kicker (e.g., A-4, A-2) might call small bet.\n- A pair of 5s or 3s with weak kicker.\n- But many hands that call are either same strength or better.\n\nBluff-catching: If you check, and opponent bluffs with missed flush draw, you can call.\n\nBut **you are ahead of many parts of their range**.\n\nBest play: **Small bet for value** — say 100 or slightly more — to extract chips from weaker Ax hands or induce bluff from missed draws.\n\nBut minimum raise is 100 (since last bet was 100 on flop, and no further bets). To *raise*, must be at least double previous bet? Not necessarily — in standard rules, on a round where prior bet was 100, a raise must be at least 100 more (i.e., total bet of 200). But here, the valid actions include "100 (raise the bet)", suggesting that **a raise of 100 is allowed**, likely because the current bet is 0 (after two checks on turn), so 100 is a legal *initial bet* on river.\n\nYes — after checks on turn, the betting round starts fresh. So current bet is 0. So you can:\n- Check (0)\n- Or make a bet (any amount ≥ big blind level — assumed to be 100, since opponent bet 100 earlier)\n\nSo 100 is a legal bet.\n\nNow, should you bet?\n\nYes — because:\n- You have strong two pair, which is likely best hand.\n- A small bet (100) can get called by:\n  - Weaker one-pair hands (A-x, 5-x, 3-x)\n  - Missed flush draws that want to see showdown (bluff-catchers)\n- It also protects against bluffs — though less important when you\'re betting.\n\nAlternatively, checking allows free showdown — but loses value.\n\nGoing all-in (-2) is too risky — over 19k chips — and would fold out all worse hands, only called by better (flushes). So bad.\n\nRaising large (e.g., 500+) same issue — scares off worse.\n\nSo best: **small value bet of 100**.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong Two Pair hand that is likely the best hand on the river. Opponent showed aggression on the flop but passivity on turn, suggesting a possible semi-bluff or marginal hand. Betting 100 extracts value from weaker pairs or bluffing missed draws, while minimizing risk against superior hands like flushes.\n\nAnswer: 100'}
